        {
          "goal": "Vulnerability analysis of Timelock",
          "focus_areas": [
            "Determine whether queued governance actions can alter oracle sources, TWAP configuration, pool pricing parameters, or other price-sensitive state without adequate delay or validation",
            "Trace queue, execution, cancellation, eta, and grace-period handling for paths that enable premature, replayed, or unexpectedly ordered price-affecting actions",
            "Assess whether arbitrary target calls or self-governed delay changes can be used to manipulate protocol prices before users can react"
          ],
          "priority": 9,
          "reasoning": "WHY NOW: Timelock is an unvisited privileged execution component and is a direct control point for governance actions that may modify price feeds or pricing parameters; reviewing it now covers a critical permission boundary while avoiding the already completed LiquidityBasedTWAP and VaderPool investigations. EXIT CRITERIA: Map every externally reachable queue, cancel, and execute path; identify all price-sensitive targets and state changes reachable through arbitrary calls; verify delay, eta, grace, replay, and self-call restrictions; conclude whether any exploitable path permits price manipulation or document that none exists.",
          "category": "aspect",
          "expected_impact": "high"
        }
      ]
